% Upper-body garments of Chapters 61 (knitted) and 62 (not knitted): the
% headings for shirts, blouses, T-shirts and tank tops, sweaters and pullovers,
% with the Section XI and chapter notes that decide between them. A library:
% it includes gri.le, and the scenario programs apparel_cbp.le (US rulings)
% and apparel_ebti.le (EU BTIs) include it.
%
% Text: HTSUS Revision 18 (2026), Section XI notes 2 and subheading note 2,
% Chapter 61 notes 1, 2(a), 4, 5, 9 and Chapter 62 notes 1, 4, 9
% (https://hts.usitc.gov/). The notes quoted are the WCO text, identical in
% the EU Combined Nomenclature; the subheadings are the six-digit HS ones.
%
% Headings modelled: 6105 (men's shirts, knitted), 6106 (women's blouses and
% shirts, knitted), 6109 (T-shirts, singlets, tank tops, knitted), 6110
% (sweaters, pullovers, sweatshirts and similar articles, knitted), 6205
% (men's shirts, woven), 6206 (women's blouses and shirts, woven). Every other
% heading — outerwear (6101, 6102, 6201, 6202), sleepwear (6107, 6108, 6207,
% 6208), brassieres (6212), other garments (6114, 6211) — is outside the model:
% a claim that the garment belongs there is answered by the office (gri.le).
%
% The facts a program reads are the ones customs rulings state about a
% garment: fabric construction and composition, closure, sleeves, openings,
% pockets and bottom, fit, neckline, straps. Where a ruling characterises a
% garment without describing it (for instance "an outer tank top"), the
% characterisation is recorded as such, with its source.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a garment* is an upper body garment; undefined.
    *a garment* is classified under the US tariff; undefined.
    *a garment* is classified under the EU tariff; undefined.
    the fabric construction of *a garment* is *a construction*; undefined.
    *a garment* contains *a number* percent of *a material*; undefined.
    all the yarn of *a garment* is metalized yarn; undefined.
    *a material* belongs to *a group*.
    *a group* is a textile group of chapter *a number*.
    the share of *a group* in *a garment* is *a number*.
    *a group* at *a number* percent outweighs *an other group* at *an other number* percent.
    the textile material of *a garment* is *a group*.
    the construction of *a garment* is *a construction*.
    the cut of *a garment* indicates *a gender*; undefined.
    *a garment* closes left over right; undefined.
    *a garment* closes right over left; undefined.
    the gender of *a garment* is *a gender*.
    *a garment* is sleeveless; undefined.
    *a garment* has an opening starting at the neckline; undefined.
    *a garment* has pockets below the waist; undefined.
    *a garment* has a means of tightening at the bottom; undefined.
    *a garment* has less than ten stitches per centimetre in each direction; undefined.
    *a garment* is loose-fitting; undefined.
    *a garment* has a collar; undefined.
    *a garment* has a high neckline; undefined.
    *a garment* is napped; undefined.
    the fabric of *a garment* is terry; undefined.
    the fabric of *a garment* is lightweight; undefined.
    *a garment* is of plain knit fabric; undefined.
    *a garment* has shoulder straps; undefined.
    the rear neckline of *a garment* falls below the nape of the neck; undefined.
    *a garment* is designed to be worn as an undergarment; undefined.
    *a garment* has a tank silhouette.
    *a garment* is a shirt.
    *a garment* is a blouse.
    *a garment* is a blouse-type garment.
    *a garment* has the fabric of a T-shirt.
    *a garment* meets the knit test of its tariff for T-shirts.
    *a garment* is a T-shirt.
    *a garment* is a tank top.
    *a garment* is a singlet.
    *a garment* is of the T-shirt family.
    heading *a heading* is a shirt heading.
    heading *a heading* is a knitted shirt heading.
    heading *a heading* excludes sleeveless garments.
    the subheading for *a group* in heading *a heading* is *a code* under table apparel.
    the subheading of *a good* is *a code*.

% GRI 6: the six-digit subheadings, by the textile material that
% predominates (Section XI subheading note 2). The subheadings of 6110 for
% wool and fine animal hair (6110.11, .12, .19) need the hair's kind, which the
% model does not read: a woollen pullover gets no subheading.
the table apparel is, with first match:
    row | material                                      | heading | code
    a1  | cotton                                        | 6105    | "6105.10"
    a2  | man-made fibres                               | 6105    | "6105.20"
    a3  | any                                           | 6105    | "6105.90"
    b1  | cotton                                        | 6106    | "6106.10"
    b2  | man-made fibres                               | 6106    | "6106.20"
    b3  | any                                           | 6106    | "6106.90"
    c1  | cotton                                        | 6109    | "6109.10"
    c2  | any                                           | 6109    | "6109.90"
    d1  | cotton                                        | 6110    | "6110.20"
    d2  | man-made fibres                               | 6110    | "6110.30"
    d3  | silk or vegetable fibres or metalized yarn    | 6110    | "6110.90"
    e1  | cotton                                        | 6205    | "6205.20"
    e2  | man-made fibres                               | 6205    | "6205.30"
    e3  | any                                           | 6205    | "6205.90"
    f1  | silk                                          | 6206    | "6206.10"
    f2  | wool fibres                                   | 6206    | "6206.20"
    f3  | cotton                                        | 6206    | "6206.30"
    f4  | man-made fibres                               | 6206    | "6206.40"
    f5  | any                                           | 6206    | "6206.90"

the knowledge base apparel includes these resources:
    gri.

the knowledge base apparel includes:

a good is within the scope of the model
    if the good is an upper body garment.

% GRI 6 and Section XI subheading note 2: the subheading of the heading, for
% the textile material of what the garment is classified as (GRI 3(b)).
the subheading of a good is a code
    if the heading of the good is a heading
    and the good is classified as consisting of a component
    and the textile material of the component is a group
    and the subheading for the group in heading the heading is the code under table apparel.

the construction of a good is a construction
    if the good is classified as consisting of a component
    and the fabric construction of the component is the construction.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Section XI note 2(A): a good of two or more textile materials is classified
% as if it consisted wholly of the one that predominates by weight over each
% other single textile material; when none predominates, as if it consisted of
% the one of the heading that occurs last in numerical order. Note 2(B)(b):
% first the chapter, then the heading within it — so the man-made fibres of
% Chapters 54 and 55 count together (2(B)(c)). Note 2(B)(a): a metalized yarn
% (heading 5605) is one textile material, whatever it is made of.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

cotton belongs to cotton.
polyester belongs to man-made fibres.
elastane belongs to man-made fibres.
spandex belongs to man-made fibres.
nylon belongs to man-made fibres.
polyamide belongs to man-made fibres.
acrylic belongs to man-made fibres.
rayon belongs to man-made fibres.
viscose belongs to man-made fibres.
modal belongs to man-made fibres.
lyocell belongs to man-made fibres.
wool belongs to wool fibres.
cashmere belongs to wool fibres.
silk belongs to silk.
flax belongs to vegetable fibres.
linen belongs to vegetable fibres.
metalized yarn belongs to metalized yarn.

silk is a textile group of chapter 50.
wool fibres is a textile group of chapter 51.
cotton is a textile group of chapter 52.
vegetable fibres is a textile group of chapter 53.
man-made fibres is a textile group of chapter 55.
metalized yarn is a textile group of chapter 56.

the share of a group in a garment is a total
    if the group is a textile group of chapter a number
    and the total is the sum of each percentage such that
        the garment contains the percentage percent of a material
        and the material belongs to the group.

the textile material of a garment is metalized yarn
    if all the yarn of the garment is metalized yarn.

the textile material of a garment is a group
    if it is not the case that
        all the yarn of the garment is metalized yarn
    and the share of the group in the garment is a total
    and the total > 0
    and for all cases in which
            the share of an other group in the garment is an other total
            and it is not the case that
                the other group is equal to the group
        it is the case that
            the group at the total percent outweighs the other group at the other total percent.

a group at a total percent outweighs an other group at an other total percent
    if the total > the other total.

a group at a total percent outweighs an other group at an other total percent
    if the total = the other total
    and the group is a textile group of chapter a number
    and the other group is a textile group of chapter an other number
    and the number > the other number.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Chapter 61 note 9, Chapter 62 note 9: left over right closure is men's,
% right over left women's, unless the cut clearly indicates the sex; a garment
% that cannot be identified goes to the women's headings.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

the gender of a garment is a gender
    if the cut of the garment indicates the gender.

the gender of a garment is men
    if it is not the case that
        the cut of the garment indicates a gender
    and the garment closes left over right.

the gender of a garment is women
    if it is not the case that
        the cut of the garment indicates a gender
    and the garment closes right over left.

the gender of a garment is women
    if it is not the case that
        the cut of the garment indicates a gender
    and it is not the case that
        the garment closes left over right
    and it is not the case that
        the garment closes right over left.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Chapter 61 note 4, Chapter 62 note 4: "Shirts" and "shirt-blouses" have long
% or short sleeves and a full or partial opening starting at the neckline;
% "blouses" are loose-fitting, may be sleeveless, with or without an opening.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

a garment is a shirt
    if it is not the case that
        the garment is sleeveless
    and the garment has an opening starting at the neckline.

a garment is a blouse
    if the garment is loose-fitting.

a garment is a blouse-type garment
    if the garment is a shirt
    otherwise the garment is a blouse.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Heading 6109. The Explanatory Note: T-shirts are lightweight knitted
% garments, not napped nor of terry, with long or short sleeves, without
% collar, without opening or fastening at the neckline, with a close-fitting or
% lower neckline; the heading also covers singlets and other vests. CBP adds,
% since HQ 950855 (1992), that the fabric be plain jersey or plain interlock.
% A tank top, for CBP, has the tank silhouette: straps, and a rear neckline
% below the nape of the neck (NY N360982).
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

a garment has the fabric of a T-shirt
    if the fabric of the garment is lightweight
    and it is not the case that
        the garment is napped
    and it is not the case that
        the fabric of the garment is terry
    and the garment meets the knit test of its tariff for T-shirts.

a garment meets the knit test of its tariff for T-shirts
    if the garment is classified under the US tariff
    and the garment is of plain knit fabric.

a garment meets the knit test of its tariff for T-shirts
    if the garment is classified under the EU tariff.

a garment is a T-shirt
    if it is not the case that
        the garment is sleeveless
    and the garment has the fabric of a T-shirt
    and it is not the case that
        the garment has a collar
    and it is not the case that
        the garment has a high neckline
    and it is not the case that
        the garment has an opening starting at the neckline.

a garment has a tank silhouette
    if the garment has shoulder straps
    and the rear neckline of the garment falls below the nape of the neck.

a garment is a tank top
    if the garment is sleeveless
    and the garment has a tank silhouette.

a garment is a singlet
    if the garment is designed to be worn as an undergarment.

a garment is of the T-shirt family
    if the garment is a T-shirt
    otherwise the garment is a tank top
    otherwise the garment is a singlet.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The headings (GRI 1). Chapter 61 note 1: only knitted articles; Chapter 62
% note 1: only articles that are not knitted.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

a garment is described by heading 6105
    if the construction of the garment is knitted
    and the gender of the garment is men
    and the garment is a shirt.

a garment is described by heading 6106
    if the construction of the garment is knitted
    and the gender of the garment is women
    and the garment is a blouse-type garment.

a garment is described by heading 6109
    if the construction of the garment is knitted
    and the garment is of the T-shirt family.

% The Explanatory Note to heading 6110: a category of knitted articles, without
% distinction as to sex, designed to cover the upper parts of the body.
a garment is described by heading 6110
    if the construction of the garment is knitted.

a garment is described by heading 6205
    if the construction of the garment is woven
    and the gender of the garment is men
    and the garment is a shirt.

a garment is described by heading 6206
    if the construction of the garment is woven
    and the gender of the garment is women
    and the garment is a blouse-type garment.

% The exclusions. Chapter 61 note 4 and Chapter 62 note 4: the shirt headings
% do not cover garments with pockets below the waist, or with a ribbed
% waistband or other means of tightening at the bottom; the knitted ones not
% those with less than 10 stitches per linear centimetre in each direction;
% 6105 and 6205 not sleeveless garments. Chapter 61 note 5: 6109 does not
% cover garments with a drawstring, ribbed waistband or other means of
% tightening at the bottom.

heading 6105 is a shirt heading.
heading 6106 is a shirt heading.
heading 6205 is a shirt heading.
heading 6206 is a shirt heading.
heading 6105 is a knitted shirt heading.
heading 6106 is a knitted shirt heading.
heading 6105 excludes sleeveless garments.
heading 6205 excludes sleeveless garments.

a garment is excluded from heading a heading
    if heading the heading is a shirt heading
    and the garment has pockets below the waist.

a garment is excluded from heading a heading
    if heading the heading is a shirt heading
    and the garment has a means of tightening at the bottom.

a garment is excluded from heading a heading
    if heading the heading is a knitted shirt heading
    and the garment has less than ten stitches per centimetre in each direction.

a garment is excluded from heading a heading
    if heading the heading excludes sleeveless garments
    and the garment is sleeveless.

a garment is excluded from heading 6109
    if the garment has a means of tightening at the bottom.

% GRI 3(a): the more specific description. Heading 6110 names a category
% ("... and similar articles") of which shirts, blouses and T-shirts are
% particular kinds; and CBP holds a loose-fitting women's top to be a blouse
% rather than a T-shirt.
heading 6105 is more specific than heading 6110,
    because "a shirt is a particular kind of the upper-body garments of heading 6110".
heading 6106 is more specific than heading 6110,
    because "a blouse or shirt is a particular kind of the upper-body garments of heading 6110".
heading 6109 is more specific than heading 6110,
    because "a T-shirt, tank top or singlet is a particular kind of the upper-body garments of heading 6110".
heading 6106 is more specific than heading 6109,
    according to CBP, as stated in HQ H325360,
    because "loose-fitting women's tops have the characteristics of blouses of Note 4 to Chapter 61, which precludes headings 6109 and 6110".
